 conference speech  even with one as as long as that and i  think with all the applause and the  standing ovations it came to something  close to an hour and a half as long as a  football match and you know you're  trying to you're speaking to the party  you're trying to keep get the party to  move in a certain direction but most  importantly you're trying to speak to  the country now what the country i'm not  going to listen to  is a kind of shopping list of then we'll  do this and then we'll do this and then  we'll do this i think you want to give a  sense of who and what you are and what  are the big things that you want to do  so for example somebody like me who  works in the mental health advocacy  field that was music to my ears there'll  be a lot of people like that that he  really was putting mental health there  something like education he was putting  out a sense of what the policy direction  is going to be and he was making some  pretty big commitments there likewise on  on the on the climate crisis and so  forth so you're putting out priorities  you're saying the kind of direction  you're going in  but i think you did that well without  you becoming a sort of you know a policy  shopping list so i certainly think it  was a very substantial speech  and a serious speech and for enough i  think the hecklers that you mentioned i  think they played into his hands because  the one thing the public want to know  when they rejected a party in elections  wrongly as they were rejected two years  ago they want to know that party's  listening and they're going to change a  bit and you know if you've got people  saying don't change don't dare change  and here's the saying you shout what we  want we are changing that's a very  positive message for the country i think  he was very ambitious wasn't he saying  that he thought that labour could be the  conservatives at the next general  election  and in doing that  he tackled all the major topics that  people are thinking 